      "plan": {
        "A": "Build the primary low-risk candidate: replace dense matrix powering with Kitamasa/characteristic-polynomial binary exponentiation. Compute x^(H-1) modulo x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN and dot coefficients of x^0..x^(N-1) with A1..AN. Use reusable buffers, high-to-low reduction, safe modular arithmetic, and validate indexing, N=1, boundary H, zeros, and large values.",
        "B": "Research a higher-risk asymptotic candidate: Bostan-Mori recurrence evaluation with fast arbitrary-modulus polynomial convolution. Since 1000000007 is not NTT-friendly, use a provably sufficient CRT/wide-arithmetic approach or reject it; do not use unverified floating-point convolution. Include a small-N fallback and retain it only if actual measurements beat the simpler recurrence method.",
        "C": "Develop a practical performance variant of polynomial powering. Focus on allocation-free/cache-friendly multiply-and-reduce kernels, safe batched or wide accumulation, and optionally race-free four-core convolution with local buffers and a serial threshold. Thread startup/merge overhead may lose on this 0.021513 s baseline, so measure rather than assume parallelism helps.",
        "G": "Integrate empirically, not additively. Check every candidate against the immutable oracle across legal edge and differential cases, then compare using the existing measurement setup. Select one complete correct implementation (or a justified size-based dispatch), preserving input/output and modulus semantics; keep A as the safe fallback and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, target, or budgets.",
        "analysis": "The valid source implements an order-N linear recurrence but uses dense N-by-N matrix exponentiation, with O(N^3 log H) work and large matrix storage. Its semantics are f_1..f_N=A and f_i=sum(P_j*f_(i-j)) modulo 1000000007. The strongest safe optimization is characteristic-polynomial evaluation in O(N^2 log H). The only measurement is a correct 0.021513 s baseline on an unknown workload mix, on four ARM64 cores and no GPU, so fixed overhead and integration effects must be measured rather than presumed."
